<<~ ahu #/entry>>

! The Boot Loader — the read before a wiki stands

''One parser holds the truth, and it runs inside a live wiki.'' A sigil's meaning lives in the tiddler
that defines it; a procedure resolves through the host's own dispatcher; a name that binds nothing
renders as its own text. None of that exists until a wiki stands.

Which leaves exactly one honest question: ''what must be read before there is anything to read with?''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-floor>>

!! The floor

A cold reader stands a wiki. That is its whole office, and the office names its limits:

# It MUST find the ''carrier frame'' — SOH, STX, ETX, EOT — because a carrier's identity and its body
  divide there, and a loader that cannot tell heading from body cannot hand a wiki its own seed.
# It MUST find the ''identity heading'' — the labelled toml fence — because the seed's address, its
  namespace and its type ride there.
# It MUST find ''worksite boundaries'' (`ahu`), because a worksite is a RECORD boundary: the parent meme
  becomes a tiddler and each `ahu` becomes a related tiddler beside it, so the boundary carries a
  structural relationship the round trip must reproduce. A loader that flattened them would hand the
  wiki one record where the author wrote several, and the relation would not survive the next write-back.
# It reads ''nothing else''. Every remaining sigil rides through as text, and the wiki resolves it once
  the wiki exists.

''The floor is a capability, not a shortfall.'' A loader that read more would hold a second opinion about
the grammar, and a second opinion is exactly what a single source of truth cannot afford.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-two-readings>>

!! Why two readers do not make two grammars

A loader and a wiki read the same bytes for different reasons, and the difference is ''scope'', never
''vocabulary'':

|!reader |!answers |!knows |
|boot loader |where does this carrier begin, end, and divide |four marks, one fence, one boundary |
|live wiki |what does each sigil DO |every tiddler that defines one |

A loader that grew opinions about `heihei` or `confidence` would be answering the wiki's question with
less evidence than the wiki has. So the loader's silence on those is not a gap to close — it is the
property that keeps one grammar from becoming two.

''The gradient carries the rest.'' Anything the loader does not frame survives as text, whole, and
reaches the wiki unaltered. Nothing is lost by not being understood early.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/not-only-a-loader>>

!! The scan set serves three offices, and only one of them is the boot

The cold scan set reads as a boot fallback and answers to that name, which invites the obvious
retirement: cut it to the floor above, since a wiki holds the grammar. ''Measured, that cut breaks
fourteen tests, and the breakages name two more offices the same set was quietly holding.''

* ''The boot floor'' — the six obligations above. Frame, heading, worksite, and pass-through.
* ''The sensorium's vocabulary.'' `wiki-sense-fold` and the memetic sensorium call the scan with NO
  grammar, reading sigil DENSITY as a signal — how thick with construct a span runs. That measure wants
  the whole vocabulary, not the frame: a text carrying `heihei` and `kahea` and `waiho` reads denser than
  one carrying none, and a loader-sized scan would report every such text as flat.
* ''The gradient's grader.'' The diagnostics path grades a construct it cannot fully read as `missing` —
  the partial rung — rather than dropping it to water. Grading needs the specific scans to know what the
  author reached for, and the generic catch-all to notice a reach it does not recognise.

''So the set stays whole, and the boot floor is a READING of it rather than a subset to cut down to.''
The retirement test — //does this exist for a spelling no live population uses?// — answers NO here:
three populations use it, and two of them are not the boot.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/conformance>>

!! Conformance

A ''conforming boot loader'' MUST: locate the four frame marks; locate the identity heading; locate
worksite boundaries; pass every other byte through unaltered; and refuse to resolve any sigil.

A conforming boot loader MUST NOT: bind a sigil name to behaviour, evaluate a procedure, follow an edge,
or grade a construct it cannot frame. Each of those belongs to the wiki, which has the tiddlers.

''Grammar conformance gets tested in a wiki.'' A test that parses outside one measures a reader the
system does not trust, and a green run there proves only that the untrusted reader agrees with itself.
The loader's own conformance — the five obligations above — gets tested outside a wiki, because that is
the only place the question means anything.

<<~/ahu>>

